---
<Layout title="Design | Andrew Smith" description="Product design across insurance, travel, payments and member services: UX process, journey mapping, workshops, benchmarking, UI and design systems." showFooterCta={true}>
<a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to content</a>
<SiteHeader />
<main id="main-content" class="doc-page">
<section class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">Design</p><h1>Product design for complex digital products.</h1><p class="hero-lede">Seven years at RAA designing end to end across insurance, travel, payments, renewals, identity and the member portal, as part of the product trio with product managers and engineers. Journey mapping, design systems, workshops and sprints are all part of one design process.</p><div class="hero-media"><Image src={heroQ} alt="Quote to buy" /><Image src={heroA} alt="My Account" /></div></section>
<div class="doc"><Toc items={toc} /><div class="doc-body">
<section class="chapter" id="shipped"><header class="chapter-head"><p class="chapter-no">01</p><h2>Shipped work</h2><p class="claim">A selection of finished products across travel, insurance, payments and membership.</p></header><div class="scroll-section"><div class="image-scroll-track-wrap"><div class="image-scroll-track"><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g0} alt="Quote to buy start" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Quote to buy start</figcaption></figure><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g1} alt="My Account" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>My Account</figcaption></figure><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g2} alt="RAA Travel home" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>RAA Travel home</figcaption></figure><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g3} alt="RAA website home" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>RAA website home</figcaption></figure><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g4} alt="Mobile hotel booking" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Mobile hotel booking</figcaption></figure><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g5} alt="Holiday search" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Holiday search</figcaption></figure><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g6} alt="Payment portal" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Payment portal</figcaption></figure><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g7} alt="Log in" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Log in</figcaption></figure><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g8} alt="Fuel map" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Fuel map</figcaption></figure><figure class="scroll-fig"><Image class="scroll-img" src={g9} alt="RAA banner" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>RAA banner</figcaption></figure></div></div></div></section>
<section class="chapter" id="ux-process"><header class="chapter-head"><p class="chapter-no">02</p><h2>UX & product process</h2><p class="claim">How experience design works within the product delivery cycle.</p><p class="lede">The experience design team works as part of the product trio — alongside product management and engineering — from the earliest conversations about what is worth building through to measuring whether it worked. This is what that contribution looks like at each stage, and how I led the team through it as manager while contributing directly as a practitioner.</p><p class="note">Covering discovery, design, delivery, and measurement across multiple product squads simultaneously.</p></header><div class="wide">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={up_discoveryDelivery} alt="Dual-track agile diagram showing discovery track focusing on value, usability, feasibility, and viability alongside delivery track focusing on scalability, reliability, performance, and maintainability" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </div><div class="sub" id="ux-process-1"><p class="section-kicker">Operating Model</p><h3>Aligning the team's process to the product delivery cycle.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>Before any piece of work begins, the team has a shared framework for how it moves through discovery and delivery. Three pathways: dual-track discovery for problems that need defining before any solution is considered, solution discovery for problems already understood, and existing components for work the design system already solves.</p>
          <p>This structure means design is always working ahead of delivery, not catching up to it. Product managers and engineering leads know what the team is working on and when it will feed into the squad. The stages below describe what happens at each point in that process.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={up_waysOfWorking} alt="Ways of working framework with pathway decision panel open showing dual-track discovery, solution discovery, and existing components pathways" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="ux-process-2"><p class="section-kicker">01 — Roadmap and Vision</p><h3>Roadmap alignment with product and senior leadership.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>Before any piece of design work begins, the team attends roadmap and vision discussions with product management and senior leadership. The goal is to understand what the business is trying to achieve, what product is prioritising and why, and where experience design can contribute ahead of squads picking up work.</p>
          <p>As manager, this is where I worked alongside senior managers across product, service design, and engineering to align the team's discovery and delivery capacity with upcoming roadmap priorities across multiple product streams.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={up_miroIdeationCritique} alt="FigJam discovery session showing problem framing, competitor analysis, and design options" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="ux-process-3"><p class="section-kicker">02 — Analytics and Insights</p><h3>Reviewing the data before scoping any discovery work.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>The team reviews product analytics alongside the insights function and product managers before scoping any discovery work. Drop-off rates, conversion data, feature adoption, NPS and CSAT trends — understanding what the data is showing informs where discovery investment will have the most impact.</p>
          <p>This is not a UX activity done in isolation. It is a shared review with product and insights to agree on what the data is telling us and where the gaps in understanding are.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={up_journeyMap1} alt="Journey explorer showing live analytics and metrics connected to each stage of the experience" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="ux-process-4"><p class="section-kicker">03 and 04 — Discovery and Workshops</p><h3>Understanding the problem before committing to a solution.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>Discovery is where the team moves from understanding what the data shows to understanding why. User research, competitor analysis, and customer feedback are synthesised to build a clear picture of the problem before any solution is considered. The team facilitates workshops and design sprints with product managers, engineers, service designers, and business stakeholders to align on problems and make decisions about direction.</p>
          <p>Discovery runs continuously alongside delivery. Findings feed back into the journey map and into planning conversations with product about what to prioritise next.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={up_miroIdeation} alt="Miro board showing current state analysis, annotated observations, and design options" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="ux-process-5"><p class="section-kicker">05 — Flow and Information Architecture</p><h3>Every path mapped before any screen is designed.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>Decision points, edge cases, and alternate flows documented alongside product managers and engineers so that requirements and design are aligned before development scope is committed. A gap found in a flow diagram costs an afternoon to fix. The same gap found in a coded feature costs a sprint.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={up_figmaFlows} alt="Figma high-level user flow and information architecture showing connected screens" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="ux-process-6"><p class="section-kicker">06 — Design and Testing</p><h3>Wireframes before high fidelity. Testing before build.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>The design system handles repeatable patterns so testing time focuses on genuinely new interactions — the decisions that have not been made before and carry the most risk if they are wrong. Finished designs include accessibility guidance, content notes, and edge case handling alongside the visual design.</p>
          <p>Usability testing runs across both discovery and delivery. Participants include people with assistive needs — screen readers, vision impairment, motor accessibility — included as standard criteria in testing recruitment, not as an afterthought.</p>
        </div>
      </div><div class="scroll-section">
        <p class="scroll-hint-label">Scroll to see more <span>→</span></p>
        <div class="image-scroll-track-wrap">
          <div class="image-scroll-track">
            <figure class="scroll-fig">
              <Image class="scroll-img" src={up_myAccount} alt="Shipped product — authenticated member portal" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
              <figcaption>Shipped product — My Account member portal</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="scroll-fig">
              <Image class="scroll-img" src={up_usertesting} alt="UserTesting session showing participant screen with tagged transcript" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
              <figcaption>Moderated usability testing session</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="scroll-fig">
              <Image class="scroll-img" src={up_travelWireframes} alt="Travel booking wireframes showing search, detail, payment, and confirmation screens" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
              <figcaption>Travel booking wireframes across key screens</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="scroll-fig">
              <Image class="scroll-img" src={up_mobileAppFlows} alt="Figma mobile app prototype flow showing connected screens" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[420,600]} sizes="420px"/>
              <figcaption>Mobile app prototype flow — logbook feature</figcaption>
            </figure>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="ux-process-7"><p class="section-kicker">07 — Delivery and Agile Ceremonies</p><h3>The designer's role within sprint delivery.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>The team works within Agile SCRUM delivery — sprint planning, backlog refinement, sprint reviews, and retrospectives. The designer's role is to keep the squad aligned on what is being built, flag when implementation is drifting from the design intent, and contribute to scope decisions alongside the product manager and engineering lead.</p>
          <p>As manager, I was responsible for the team's delivery cadence across multiple squads simultaneously — tracking progress, resolving blockers, and ensuring the ways of working between design and engineering were producing the right outcomes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={up_figmaRenewals} alt="Figma canvas showing design screens across desktop and mobile ready for engineering handover" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="ux-process-8"><p class="section-kicker">08 — Measurement</p><h3>Closing the loop between delivery and the next decision.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>Shipping is where the hypothesis gets tested at scale. Benchmarking runs at regular intervals after delivery — tracking effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, and desirability so the data tells a story about direction over time. Results feed back into the journey map and into the next planning cycle with product.</p>
          <p>The loop closes here. What the measurement reveals becomes the input to the next round of analytics review and discovery planning. This is what makes design a continuous contribution to the product rather than a one-time delivery.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={up_uxBenchmarking} alt="Usability benchmarking dashboard showing effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, and desirability metrics" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="ux-process-9"><p class="section-kicker">AI in the Process</p><h3>Reducing overhead to increase capacity for judgment.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>AI tools are now part of how the team works at multiple stages — from research synthesis and journey map generation in discovery, to spec documentation and accessibility checking in delivery. The goal is reducing the overhead that competes with the work that requires judgment, not replacing the judgment itself.</p>
          <p>The orchestration layer connected the design system, journey maps, research repository, and live product data into a single queryable source — so knowledge was accessible across a distributed team without manual search across four tools. See the <a href="#ai-prototyping">AI Tooling</a> page for the full picture.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={up_orchestration} alt="Experience Design operating model showing AI orchestration layer connecting all tools and knowledge sources" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div></section>
<section class="chapter" id="ways-of-working"><header class="chapter-head"><p class="chapter-no">03</p><h2>Ways of working</h2><p class="claim">Ways of working for a UX team.</p><p class="lede">The most common failure mode for a design team is not a lack of talent. It is a lack of structure. Work arrives without a clear pathway. Designers make isolated decisions that conflict with each other. Knowledge sits in individual heads and disappears when someone leaves. The team works hard and still delivers inconsistently, because the system around the work has not been built with the same care as the work itself.</p><p class="lede">An operating model solves this. Not by adding process for its own sake, but by making the right decisions easier to make consistently: who needs to be involved at each stage, what pathway a piece of work should take, when to escalate, how decisions get documented, and how the team reviews work collectively rather than in isolation.</p></header><div class="meta-strip">
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Scope</p><p class="meta-value">Experience Design department</p></div>
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Role</p><p class="meta-value">UX Manager</p></div>
        <div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">Teams</p><p class="meta-value">UX, Service Design, Content, BA</p></div>
      </div><div class="wide">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={sp_wow1} alt="Ways of working framework showing three delivery pathways: dual-track discovery, solution discovery, and existing components , with continuous loop" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </div><div class="sub" id="ways-of-working-1"><p class="section-kicker">Why Ways of Working Matter</p><h3>The team is the system. And like any system, it works best when it is designed.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        
        
        <div class="summary-main">
          <p>Managing a team of senior designers across multiple product squads , alongside service designers, content managers, and business analysts , means that the manager cannot be in every conversation. The team needs to be able to make good decisions independently, know when to escalate, and collaborate effectively without waiting for direction on every piece of work.</p>
          <p>That requires explicit structure. Not because senior designers cannot think for themselves, but because without shared expectations, every designer develops their own interpretation of what good looks like, when to involve others, and what level of fidelity is appropriate for a given stage of work. Over time, that divergence costs more in rework and alignment than any individual process overhead would have.</p>
          <p>The ways of working framework below defines three delivery pathways, each matched to a different type of work. Work that needs new discovery before any design begins takes the dual-track path. Work where the problem is understood takes the solution discovery path. Work the design system already solves takes the existing components path, meaning no new design work, just governance and guidance.</p>
          <p>The pathway decision is made collectively in the Studio session at the start of every piece of work, not by the manager alone, and not by the individual designer. That collective ownership of the process is what makes it actually used rather than documented and ignored.</p>
        </div>
      </div><div class="wide">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={sp_wow2} alt="Ways of working framework with pathway decision panel open showing the three pathways and why each one exists" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </div><div class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">The Studio Model</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>The Studio is a standing weekly session where the full team brings work at any stage: early sketches, mid-process decisions, near-final designs, for collective critique. It is the single most important practice for maintaining consistent quality across a distributed team.</p>
          <p>Without a shared critique space, quality depends entirely on whether individual designers happen to have the right conversations with the right people at the right time. The Studio, the team develops a shared sense of what good looks like, informed by the collective experience of UX, service design, content, and business analysis all in the same room.</p>
          <p>The Studio also functions as a safety net. When a team member went on leave mid-project, the rest of the team already understood the work because it had been through the Studio. The system absorbed the disruption without anything being dropped. That is the actual test of whether a practice is real or decorative.</p>
        </div>
      </div><div class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">AI Orchestration Layer</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>As the team reduced in size, the question became how to maintain quality and knowledge access without adding headcount. Individual designers in product squads were physically distributed across the organisation. Knowledge was fragmented across tools. A designer starting a new piece of work had no reliable way to know what research existed for that product area, what the design system already covered, or what decisions had been documented previously.</p>
          <p>The orchestration layer connected these sources. A Slackbot connected to the design system, journey maps, research repository, live products, and internal documentation, so a designer could ask a question in the channel they were already working in and get an answer synthesised from all available sources. The knowledge layer also connected to WCAG accessibility guidelines, solution architecture standards, brand guidelines, and legal requirements, meaning the team could surface constraints from across the organisation rather than working in isolation from the broader standards they were expected to design against.</p>
          <p>The diagram below shows the full architecture: business and management inputs on the left, the prioritisation and routing layer in the centre, the AI layer at the base connecting all knowledge sources, and delivered outputs and reporting on the right. The result was a smaller team maintaining the same quality and decision-making speed as a larger one , because the institutional knowledge was finally accessible.</p>
        </div>
      </div><div class="wide">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={sp_orchestration} alt="Experience Design operating model showing business inputs, prioritisation layer, EXD Studio, Claude AI orchestration connecting all tools and knowledge sources, and delivered outputs" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </div></div></section>
<section class="chapter" id="workshops"><header class="chapter-head"><p class="chapter-no">04</p><h2>Workshops & design sprints</h2><p class="claim">Facilitating discovery — getting the right people in the room to solve the right problem.</p><p class="lede">Workshops and design sprints are how a cross-functional team moves from individual opinions to a shared, evidence-based decision. Running them well means the right structure, strict timeboxing, and leaving with something the team can act on.</p><p class="note">Two design sprints facilitated using Google Ventures methodology, alongside ongoing ideation workshops, requirements gathering sessions, and cross-functional discovery work.</p></header><div class="sub" id="workshops-1"><p class="section-kicker">What is a Design Sprint?</p><h3>A structured, time-boxed process for moving from problem to tested solution.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>The Google Ventures Design Sprint is a framework for answering critical business questions through rapid design and user testing. It brings together a cross-functional group including product, engineering, design, and stakeholders, and moves them through problem definition, ideation, prototyping, and testing in a compressed timeframe.</p>
          <p>The value is not the speed. It is the discipline. By timeboxing every activity and running individual work in parallel rather than group discussion, the sprint surfaces what the team actually knows, what it assumes, and where the evidence points. A nominated Decider makes the final call. The Facilitator keeps the process honest.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={ws_sprintDiagram} alt="Five-day design sprint diagram showing Monday Map, Tuesday Sketch, Wednesday Decide, Thursday Prototype, Friday Test" loading="eager" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="workshops-2"><p class="section-kicker">Sprint 1 — QTB Payments</p><h3>RAA Quote to Buy — Payments Design Sprint</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>RAA's online insurance purchase platform had a conversion problem at the payment stage. The business assumed the fix was adding more payment methods. Before committing delivery capacity to that assumption, a design sprint was run to find out whether it was actually true.</p>
          <p>The sprint synthesised five years of existing research including usability studies, GA4 analytics, journey maps, and third-party UX diagnostics into a single structured reference before any ideation began. Two senior leadership sessions were built in — the first to align on the right problems, the second to review prototypes and determine what would proceed to delivery.</p>
          <p>The synthesis revealed the real blocker: around 38 to 40 percent of quotes were ending as deferred because RAA could not verify the member's identity to proceed. The problem was member matching, not payment method. That finding redirected the product roadmap. Authenticated quote-to-buy is now live for motor insurance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={ws_qtbWall} alt="QTB sprint insight wall showing HMW statements, personas, and key insights" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
