<section class="chapter" id="benchmarking"><header class="chapter-head"><p class="chapter-no">06</p><h2>UX benchmarking</h2><p class="claim">Benchmarking digital product quality over time.</p><p class="lede">A single usability study tells you what is happening now. A benchmarking program tells you whether things are getting better or worse. Tracking effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, and desirability at regular intervals gives product and engineering teams evidence of whether changes are actually improving the experience — or just shipping features.</p><p class="note">Built from scratch at RAA and run across multiple product areas as a continuous measurement practice.</p></header><div class="meta-strip">
        <div><p class="meta-label">Scope</p><p class="meta-value">Insurance, travel, membership, identity</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Role</p><p class="meta-value">Senior UX Design Lead</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Tools</p><p class="meta-value">UserTesting.com, Optimal Workshop, custom metrics framework</p></div>
      </div><div class="wide">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={rb_uxBenchmarking} alt="UX benchmarking dashboard showing effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, and desirability metrics across products and time periods" decoding="async" widths={[720,1080,1440]} sizes="(max-width:78rem) 100vw, 78rem"/>
      </div><div class="sub" id="benchmarking-1"><p class="section-kicker">The Framework</p><h3>Four dimensions. Tracked consistently. Compared over time.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        
        
        <div class="summary-body">
          <p>The benchmarking framework measures four dimensions across each product area. Effectiveness — can users complete the task at all? Efficiency — how long does it take and how many errors occur? Satisfaction — how do users rate the experience? Desirability — does the experience reflect the brand qualities the business wants to be associated with?</p>
          <p>Each study is run to a standardised protocol so results can be compared across time periods. A change in score means something because the methodology is consistent. When a metric improves after a delivery, there is evidence the change worked. When it does not improve, there is a signal that something else needs investigating. The data feeds directly into the journey map, keeping prioritisation grounded in measurement rather than assumption.</p>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="benchmarking-2"><p class="section-kicker">Research Operations</p><h3>Built from scratch — repository, protocols, and practice.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>Before the research operations function existed at RAA, findings were produced for individual projects and then essentially lost. No shared repository. No way to build on previous work. Each new project started from scratch.</p>
          <p>Research operations introduced a structured repository tagging findings to specific products and stages of the experience, a standardised testing process so findings could be compared across studies over time, and a regular benchmarking cadence that gave product and engineering teams a consistent signal about experience quality. The infrastructure changed how the organisation made decisions — from opinion-led to evidence-led.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={rb_usertesting} alt="UserTesting session showing participant screen with tagged transcript and observation notes" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div><div class="sub" id="benchmarking-3"><p class="section-kicker">Connected to Journey Maps</p><h3>Measurement that feeds back into prioritisation.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>Benchmarking results feed directly into the journey map at the stage of the experience where each study ran. When a metric shifts — a drop in completion rate, a rise in error frequency, a fall in satisfaction score — the map shows exactly which stage is affected and what the current prioritised responses are.</p>
          <p>That connection between measurement and action is what makes the system useful. Without it, benchmarking produces reports that get filed. With it, benchmarking shapes what the team works on next.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sub-media">
          <Image class="aside-img" src={rb_journeyMap1} alt="Journey explorer showing live metrics and benchmarking data feeding back into prioritised solutions" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[480,720]} sizes="(max-width:1200px) 30vw, 480px"/>
        </div>
      </div></div></section>
<section class="chapter" id="testing"><header class="chapter-head"><p class="chapter-no">07</p><h2>Research & testing</h2><p class="claim">Testing at the end checks a decision after it is expensive to change.</p><p class="lede">Research is how design converts assumption into evidence. The usability benchmarking dashboard below measures the experience across four dimensions: effectiveness, efficiency, satisfaction, and desirability, tracked over time so the team can see whether things are actually improving.</p></header><div class="meta-strip">
        {rt_highlights.map((item) => (<div class="meta-item"><p class="meta-label">{item.label}</p><p class="meta-value">{item.value}</p></div>))}
      </div><div class="wide">
        <Image class="lead-image" src={rt_uxBenchmarkingImage} alt="Usability benchmarking dashboard for insurance quote , effectiveness 39/1, efficiency 4 min average, satisfaction, and desirability word cloud" decoding="async" widths={[720, 1080, 1440]} sizes="(max-width: 78rem) 100vw, 78rem" />
      </div><div class="sub" id="testing-1"><p class="section-kicker">Research Operations</p><h3>Built a formal research practice at RAA from nothing.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        
        
        <div class="summary-main">
          <p>When I joined RAA, design decisions were made on stakeholder opinion, past convention, or whoever spoke most confidently in the room. There was no research repository, no shared testing methodology, no way to track whether the experience was improving over time.</p>
          <p>Over five years, I built Research Operations from the ground up, including a research repository, team onboarding documentation, a UX metrics framework, and the testing cadences that made continuous validation part of how the team worked rather than an event scheduled before launch.</p>
          <p class="summary-meta"><strong>Tools:</strong> UserTesting.com, Optimal Workshop, Figma prototypes, analytics synthesis</p>
          <p class="summary-meta"><strong>Scope:</strong> Moderated and unmoderated usability testing, benchmarking studies, confidence testing, research synthesis</p>
        </div>
      </div><div class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">The Benchmarking Dashboard</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>The dashboard above shows quantitative research data for the RAA Insurance home and contents quote flow. Effectiveness measures whether users completed the task successfully (39 success, 1 fail). Efficiency shows time-on-task distribution with a mean of 4 minutes. Satisfaction and usefulness are measured via post-task Likert scales. Desirability captures the emotional quality of the experience through word choice.</p>
          <p>This format makes the research actionable and comparable over time. When a feature ships, the next benchmarking study either confirms the improvement or surfaces new issues, giving the team a data-backed case for what to prioritise next.</p>
        </div>
      </div><div class="editorial-section">
        <div class="section-rail"><p class="section-kicker">User Research</p></div>
        <div class="story-block">
          <p>Quantitative benchmarking is only half the picture. Moderated usability sessions, ethnographic interviews, and field research provide the qualitative layer: the reasons behind the numbers, the language users actually use, the mental models that the interface does or does not match.</p>
          <p>Research findings were synthesised into personas, journey maps, and insight repositories that the whole team could reference, turning individual research sessions into organisational knowledge rather than notes on someone's hard drive.</p>
        </div>
      </div><div class="wide">
        <Image class="supporting-image" src={rt_raaPersonaImage} alt="User persona for RAA Travel, Ali, 32, young professional RAA member" loading="lazy" decoding="async" widths={[720, 1080, 1440]} sizes="(max-width: 78rem) 100vw, 78rem" />
      </div></div></section>
<section class="chapter" id="ui-design"><header class="chapter-head"><p class="chapter-no">08</p><h2>UI design</h2><p class="claim">UI design across digital products.</p><p class="lede">End-to-end UI design across insurance, membership, payments, identity, travel, and mobile. Designed within Agile squads alongside product managers, engineers, and content from discovery through to shipped product. Starting in front-end development means working with engineers is a peer conversation, not a handover.</p><p class="note">Interactions and interfaces built for 800,000+ RAA members. Design system components built for engineering adoption from day one.</p></header><div class="meta-strip">
        <div><p class="meta-label">Scope</p><p class="meta-value">Travel, Insurance, Membership, Identity, Payments, Mobile</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Role</p><p class="meta-value">Senior UX Design Lead, UX Manager</p></div>
        <div><p class="meta-label">Tools</p><p class="meta-value">Expert in Figma, design system governance, WCAG accessibility</p></div>
      </div><div class="sub" id="ui-design-1"><p class="section-kicker">Shipped Products</p><h3>Public-facing digital products used by members every day.</h3><div class="sub-grid">
        <div class="sub-text">
          
          
          <p>Designed and iterated the RAA Travel platform from the initial rebuild through to its current state covering flights, stays, holidays, cruises, car hire, and experiences, across desktop and mobile. Led the UX design for the RAA website redesign working with external agencies and internal engineering.</p>
          <p>The quote and buy journey covers motor, home, and landlord insurance. Multiple rounds of usability testing, prototype iteration, and post-delivery benchmarking across each product area. Accessibility reviewed against WCAG 2.1 standards throughout, with ongoing audits through the Vision Australia partnership.</p>
        </div>
